Q: What is CSRF? When do we enable/disable it in Spring?

CSRF = Cross-Site Request Forgery.
A malicious site tricks a logged-in user’s browser into sending an unwanted request to your site, and the browser **automatically attaches cookies**.

────────────────────────────────────────
Example

Victim is logged into bank (session cookie present).
Bank endpoint: POST /transfer

Attacker page:
<form action="https://bank.com/transfer" method="POST">
  <input type="hidden" name="toAccount" value="attacker123">
  <input type="hidden" name="amount" value="1000">
</form>
<script>document.forms[0].submit();</script>

Browser sends Cookie: JSESSIONID=... automatically → server trusts it → unwanted transfer.

────────────────────────────────────────
How to prevent

1) CSRF token (most common)
Server issues random token; client must send it on state-changing requests.
Missing/wrong token → reject.

POST /transfer
Cookie: JSESSIONID=ABC123
X-CSRF-TOKEN: token123

2) SameSite cookies
SameSite=Lax or Strict reduces cross-site cookie sending.

3) Origin / Referer checks
Reject unexpected cross-origin POSTs.

────────────────────────────────────────
CSRF vs XSS

| CSRF                         | XSS                                |
| ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| Forged request from browser  | Injected malicious JS              |
| Exploits site’s trust in browser cookies | Exploits user’s trust in page |
| Needs logged-in cookie session | Auth not always required         |
| Tokens / SameSite            | Validate input, encode output      |

────────────────────────────────────────
Spring Security

- Browser session/cookie apps: CSRF **enabled by default** (good)
- Many JWT REST APIs: often `http.csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())`

Why disable for JWT APIs?
If auth is `Authorization: Bearer <jwt>` (not auto-attached by browser), classic cookie CSRF usually doesn’t apply.

⚠️ Important caveat:
If you store JWT in a **cookie**, CSRF risk returns → protect like session apps (token/SameSite).

Session vs JWT (cookie vs header):

| Feature                    | Session cookie auth | JWT in Authorization header |
| -------------------------- | ------------------- | --------------------------- |
| Browser auto-sends creds   | Yes                 | No                          |
| CSRF concern               | Yes                 | Usually low                 |
| CSRF protection needed     | Yes                 | Usually no*                 |

*Unless token is cookie-based.

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview answers

When enable CSRF?
“When auth relies on cookies/sessions that the browser sends automatically. CSRF tokens prove the request came from your app, not a malicious site.”

Why many Spring Boot REST APIs disable CSRF?
“Because they use stateless Bearer JWT in the Authorization header, which browsers don’t auto-attach cross-site. If we used cookie auth, we’d keep CSRF on.”
